---
title: "Guest API: Domains & Currency"
description: Public (unauthenticated) endpoints for TLD listings, domain pricing, availability checks and currency formatting.
---

The endpoints on this page belong to the **guest** API role. They require no
authentication and no CSRF token, and can be called from any server-side
integration (for example a WordPress price list or a custom domain search form).

All endpoints follow the standard pattern `/api/guest/{module}/{action}`, accept
`GET` or `POST`, and take parameters as JSON or form data. See the
[API Reference](/extensions-and-development/api/#response-format) for the general response and error format.

{% aside type="note" %}
Successful calls return `{"result": ..., "error": null}`. On failure, `result`
is `null` and `error` contains `message` and `code`. Some application errors are
returned with HTTP 200, so always check the `error` field as well as the status
code.
{% /aside %}

## Domains (`servicedomain`)

TLD values are always written **with the leading dot**, e.g. `.com`, `.se`.

### `guest/servicedomain/tlds`

Lists all **active** TLDs with their pricing.

| Parameter        | Type | Required | Description |
|------------------|------|----------|-------------|
| `allow_register` | bool | No       | If present, filters by registration flag: `1` returns only registrable TLDs, `0` returns only non-registrable TLDs. Omit to disable filtering. |
| `allow_transfer` | bool | No       | If present, filters by transfer flag: `1` returns only transferable TLDs, `0` returns only non-transferable TLDs. Omit to disable filtering. |

{% aside type="caution" %}
The filters are enabled by the *presence* of the parameter, and the value is cast to boolean.
Sending `allow_register=0` or `allow_register=false` still applies the filter, but matches TLDs where registration is disabled.
Omit the parameter entirely to disable it.
{% /aside %}

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/servicedomain/tlds
```

```json
{
  "result": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "tld": ".com",
      "price_registration": "11.99",
      "price_renew": "11.99",
      "price_transfer": "11.99",
      "active": 1,
      "allow_register": 1,
      "allow_transfer": 1,
      "require_transfer_code": false,
      "min_years": 1,
      "periods": null
    }
  ],
  "error": null
}
```

| Field                   | Description |
|-------------------------|-------------|
| `price_registration`    | Price per year for registration, in the **default currency**. |
| `price_renew`           | Price per year for renewal. |
| `price_transfer`        | Price for transfer. |
| `require_transfer_code` | Whether an EPP/auth code is required for transfers. |
| `min_years`             | Minimum registration period in years. |
| `periods`               | Allowed registration periods in years, or `null` if not restricted. |

Prices are returned unformatted and in the system's default currency. Use
[`guest/currency/format`](#guestcurrencyformat) to convert and format them.

### `guest/servicedomain/pricing`

Returns pricing for a single TLD. The response object has the same fields as
one entry from [`tlds`](#guestservicedomaintlds).

| Parameter | Type   | Required | Description |
|-----------|--------|----------|-------------|
| `tld`     | string | Yes      | TLD including the leading dot, e.g. `.se`. |

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/servicedomain/pricing -d 'tld=.com'
```

**Errors**

| Message         | Cause |
|-----------------|-------|
| `TLD not found` | The TLD does not exist in FOSSBilling. |

{% aside type="note" %}
Unlike `tlds`, this endpoint does not check whether the TLD is active.
{% /aside %}

### `guest/servicedomain/check`

Checks whether a domain is available for registration. The result comes from
the registrar module configured for the TLD, so a registrar must be set up for
the answer to be accurate.

| Parameter | Type   | Required | Description |
|-----------|--------|----------|-------------|
| `sld`     | string | Yes      | Second-level part of the domain, e.g. `example` for `example.com`. |
| `tld`     | string | Yes      | TLD including the leading dot. |

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/servicedomain/check \
  -d 'sld=example' -d 'tld=.com'
```

Returns `true` when the domain is available:

```json
{ "result": true, "error": null }
```

A registered domain does **not** return `false`. It returns an error instead:

```json
{
  "result": null,
  "error": { "message": "Domain is not available.", "code": 9999 }
}
```

**Errors**

| Message | Cause |
|---------|-------|
| `Domain is not available.` | The domain is already registered. |
| `Domain example is invalid` | The `sld` failed validation (the message includes the submitted value). |
| `Domain availability could not be determined. TLD is not configured.` | Unknown TLD. |
| `Domain availability could not be determined. TLD is not active.` | TLD exists but is inactive. |

Registrar modules may return their own error messages when the registry cannot be
reached, for example `Domain availability check failed. Please try again later.`

{% aside type="caution" title="All errors use code 9999" %}
Every error above, including `Domain is not available.`, is returned with
`code: 9999` and HTTP 200. Your integration cannot tell a taken domain from a
configuration or registry error by the code alone. Compare the message, or
treat any error as "not available" and log it.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="caution" title="Rate limited" %}
This endpoint is rate limited per client IP (`domain_lookup_ip` limiter).
Server-side integrations that proxy many visitors through a single IP should
cache results or throttle requests.
{% /aside %}

### `guest/servicedomain/can_be_transferred`

Checks whether a domain can be transferred in.

| Parameter | Type   | Required | Description |
|-----------|--------|----------|-------------|
| `sld`     | string | Yes      | Second-level part of the domain. |
| `tld`     | string | Yes      | TLD including the leading dot. |

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/servicedomain/can_be_transferred \
  -d 'sld=example' -d 'tld=.com'
```

Returns `true` if the domain can be transferred.

**Errors**

| Message | Cause |
|---------|-------|
| `TLD is missing` / `SLD is missing` | A required parameter was not sent. |
| `TLD is not configured.` | Unknown TLD. |
| `TLD is not active.` | TLD exists but is inactive. |
| `Domain cannot be transferred.` | The registrar reported that the domain cannot be transferred. |

This endpoint shares the `domain_lookup_ip` rate limit with `check`.

## Currency (`currency`)

### `guest/currency/get_pairs`

Returns the configured currencies as an object mapping currency code to title.
Takes no parameters.

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/currency/get_pairs
```

```json
{
  "result": {
    "USD": "US Dollar",
    "EUR": "Euro"
  },
  "error": null
}
```

### `guest/currency/get`

Returns details of a currency, including its `conversion_rate`.

| Parameter | Type   | Required | Description |
|-----------|--------|----------|-------------|
| `code`    | string | No       | Currency code, e.g. `SEK`. If omitted, the **default currency** is returned. |

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/currency/get -d 'code=SEK'
```

**Errors**

| Message               | Cause |
|-----------------------|-------|
| `Currency not found.` | Unknown currency code, or no default currency is set. |

### `guest/currency/format`

Formats a price as a localized string, optionally converting it from the
default currency.

| Parameter          | Type   | Required | Default            | Description |
|--------------------|--------|----------|--------------------|-------------|
| `price`            | float  | No       | `0`                | Amount in the default currency. |
| `code`             | string | No       | default currency   | Target currency code. |
| `convert`          | bool   | No       | `true`             | Multiply `price` by the target currency's `conversion_rate`. |
| `without_currency` | bool   | No       | `false`            | Return only the number, without currency symbol. |

Formatting follows the active locale of the request.

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/currency/format \
  -d 'price=11.99' -d 'code=SEK'
```

## Example: building a public price list

```bash
# 1. Fetch all TLDs that can be registered
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/servicedomain/tlds -d 'allow_register=1'

# 2. Get the default currency (for symbol / formatting)
curl -s -X POST https://example.com/api/guest/currency/get
```

A single `tlds` call returns prices for every TLD, so there is no need to call
`pricing` per TLD. Cache the response on your side; prices change rarely.
